document.write('<li class="rss-item"><a class="rss-item" href="http://www.gadgetreview.com/2011/01/gorilla-glass-hard-to-break-intentionally.html" target="_blank">Gorilla Glass, Hard To Break Intentionally</a><br />');
document.write('<p style=\"text-align: center\"><object width=\"600\" height=\"400\"><param name=\"movie\" value=\"http://www.youtube.com/v/XyFbA7-JgGw&rel=0&color1=0xd6d6d6&color2=0xf0f0f0\"></param><param name=\"wmode\" value=\"transparent\"></param><embed src=\"http://www.youtube.com/v/XyFbA7-JgGw&rel=0&color1=0xd6d6d6&color2=0xf0f0f0\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" wmode=\"transparent\" width=\"600\" height=\"400\"></object></p> <p>Low key as it may be in the world of consumer tech, the glass we use in any of our gadgets isn&#8217;t going away anytime soon. In fact, we&#8217;ll be using more and more, especially with the proliferation of touchscreen technology. Yet glass as a surface is surprisingly easy to break. One drop on the ground and crack. Broken, shattered, or just cracked enough that the picture doesn&#8217;t go through. Hell, just a few weeks ago I brought my brother&#8217;s iPod Touch to be repaired because he managed to drop it on a corner, and cracked the screen. It was brand new. <a href=\"http://www.gadgetreview.com/2011/01/gorilla-glass-hard-to-break-intentionally.html#more-79084\" class=\"more-link\">(more&#8230;)</a></p>');
